--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0336 revived Flag 5 (semantic isolation), which had been returning
`skipped` on every run. The fix merges with the flag held inert via
`--skip-semantic-flag` in `dvc.yaml`'s `extend.cmd`, because activating it as
shipped would remove 361 works on a threshold no human has ever seen produce
output.

`sigma: 2` was set on a smaller corpus under MiniLM. The embedding model was
swapped to BGE-M3 **15 days after the flag silently died**, so the threshold
deciding 361 removals has never been calibrated against anything it produced.

## What the shipped setting would remove

361 net new removals, reproduced independently of the implementing agent.
Nothing precious by citation: **zero works cited >= 50**, max 43, median 0 — the
core subset (~2,300 papers cited >= 50) loses nothing. Most are genuine harvest
false positives (doctoral viva procedures, Ce-Zr nickel catalysis, fractional
differential equations, mycotoxins in animal feed, Irish negative equity).

But 45 of the 361 titles carry climate vocabulary, and these are in scope:

- *From small to insignificant: Climate impact of the Kyoto Protocol with and
  without the US* (2001, 39 cites)
- *International Carbon Finance and EcoSecurities* (2008)
- *Official Documents — Agreement with France for Donation to the Adaptation
  Fund* (2013)
- *The feasibility of ambitious climate agreements: Norway as an early test
  case* (2002)
- *COP27 climate change conference: urgent action needed for Africa and the
  world* (2022)

A paper titled *International Carbon Finance* culled as a semantic outlier is a
calibration failure, not a filter working.

## The language gradient — the real defect

Baseline removal rate 1.09% of 33,057 candidates. By language, shipped setting:

| lang | candidates | removed | rate | vs baseline |
|------|-----------|---------|------|-------------|
| es | 449 | 20 | 4.45% | 4.1x |
| ko | 87 | 3 | 3.45% | 3.2x |
| pt | 608 | 20 | 3.29% | 3.0x |
| fr | 354 | 10 | 2.82% | 2.6x |
| ru | 119 | 3 | 2.52% | 2.3x |
| en | 30,225 | 288 | 0.95% | 0.9x |

The centroid is built from a 91.6%-English corpus, so distance-from-centroid
partly measures *not-in-English* rather than *off-topic*. Non-English works are
culled at 2.3-4.1x the English rate — and that multilingual tail is the
dependent variable of `docs/research-note-multilingual.md`. Activating the flag
as shipped would thin the population that note studies, while the data paper
reports a six-flag filter whose sixth flag has an unmeasured language gradient.

## Calibration sweep (read-only, frozen corpus)

The per-language arm computes both centroid and mean/std within language,
falling back to the global arm below n=30.

| variant | flagged | net rm | climate-vocab culled | on-topic 6 culled | es | pt | fr | ko | en |
|---------|---------|--------|---------------------|-------------------|----|----|----|----|----|
| global s=2 *(shipped)* | 1276 | 361 | 45 | 6/6 | 4.1x | 3.0x | 2.6x | 3.1x | 0.9x |
| global s=3 | 238 | 38 | 1 | 1/6 | 9.7x | 4.3x | 4.9x | 0x | 0.7x |
| global s=4 | 39 | 5 | 0 | 0/6 | 14.7x | 10.9x | 18.6x | 0x | 0.4x |
| per-lang s=2 | 1324 | 385 | 48 | 6/6 | 1.1x | 0.4x | 1.9x | 1.0x | 1.0x |
| **per-lang s=3** | 238 | **45** | 4 | 1/6 | 0x | 1.2x | 2.1x | 0x | 1.0x |
| per-lang s=4 | 40 | 4 | 1 | 1/6 | 0x | 0x | 23.3x | 0x | 0.8x |

Three findings:

1. **The per-language centroid fixes the gradient at its source.** At s=2 it
   collapses es 4.1x -> 1.1x, pt 3.0x -> 0.4x, ko 3.2x -> 1.0x, en 0.9x -> 1.0x.
2. **Raising sigma against a global centroid makes the bias worse**, not better:
   global s=4 gives es 14.7x, pt 10.9x, fr 18.6x, because the extreme tail of a
   91.6%-English centroid distribution is almost entirely non-English. Sigma is
   the wrong lever for the gradient; the centroid is the right one.
3. **s=4 empties the flag**: 4 removals of 32,938 (0.012%). A sixth flag that
   culls four papers is decorative, and harder to defend in the paper than
   honestly reporting five.

Caveat: at s=3 and s=4 the per-language ratios rest on 0-7 works per language,
so those multipliers are noise (fr 23.3x at s=4 is one paper of 354). They are
not signal.

## Options

- **A. Per-language centroid at s=3** (recommended). 45 removals, flat gradient
  (en 1.0x, pt 1.2x, es 0x), 4 climate-vocab works culled against 45 shipped. A
  flag that filters without a language gradient.
- **B. Retire Flag 5.** Defensible: Flag 6 (LLM relevance) already flags 11,244
  works and does topical filtering better; Flag 5's marginal contribution at s=3
  is 45 works of 33,000. The paper would describe five flags, which is what the
  artifact has actually been for months. Cheapest and most honest option.
- **C. Per-language centroid at s=2.** Fixes the gradient but still culls all six
  named on-topic works. Not recommended.

Under every variant but global s=4 the *Adaptation Fund* document is culled. It
is an `Official Documents` record, so that is an abstract-quality problem, not a
threshold problem — no sigma fixes it. Handle separately or accept.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/filter_flags.py` — `flag_semantic_outlier` (centroid and threshold)
- `scripts/harvest/corpus_filter.py` — `load_embeddings`, `_flag5_subset`
- `config/corpus_filter.yaml` — diagnostic mode, centroid scope, language floor,
  `min_coverage`
- `dvc.yaml` — `extend.cmd` carries `--skip-semantic-flag`; removing it activates
- `docs/research-note-multilingual.md` — the note whose signal Flag 5 removes on
- `deliverables/data-paper/data-paper.qmd` and the revision letter — both
  describe six flags while the artifact has five

## Actions

1. Implement diagnostic mode with a per-language centroid, a documented
   small-language fallback and unit tests, without changing the live config or
   DVC stage.
2. In a separate change, configure diagnostic mode, remove
   `--skip-semantic-flag`, rebuild the v3 outputs, and prove that the diagnostic
   mask removes zero works.
3. Record the v3 row count and publish `semantic_outlier_dist` in the v3 data
   contract. The tagged v2 paper and archive remain unchanged.

## Test

- A test asserting the language gradient stays inside a stated band after
  calibration. The defect this ticket exists for is invisible to any count
  assertion; only a per-language rate comparison sees it.
- If B, a test asserting the flag is absent from `FLAG_COLUMNS` and that the
  prose count matches.

## Verification

- [x] Diagnostic option implemented; the recorded counterfactual calibration
      shows the per-language correction removes the global language gradient
- [x] `refined_works.csv` remains byte-identical at 33,344 rows
- [x] V3 data-paper source and deposit contract describe five removal flags plus
      the published semantic-distance diagnostic; tagged v2 remains immutable
- [x] `--skip-semantic-flag` removed after author sign-off and zero-removal replay

## Invariants

- The published v2 corpus stays frozen at `rdj26561-revision1`. V3 development
  is unfrozen (author, 2026-09-16), but no Flag 5 activation occurs before its
  separate calibration/sign-off gate.
- No weakening of acceptance thresholds.
- 0362 should land before any activation: `run_corpus_pipeline.sh` auto-pushes
  `dvc.lock` to main, so an activated flag would execute with no checkpoint.

## Exit criteria

- Flag 5 publishes a per-language semantic distance, removes no work, and the
  v3 paper and deposit contract distinguish it from the five removal flags.
